1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,344
Chapter seven — scale and cost.

2
00:00:03,404 --> 00:00:09,347
The question is never "does it work".
It is "when does it stop working".

3
00:00:09,407 --> 00:00:15,350
Push the workload and watch.

4
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:27,903
Records up: storage, cost and
every node metric move together.

5
00:00:27,963 --> 00:00:36,215
Queries up: the read paths start
to bind before the write paths do.

6
00:00:36,275 --> 00:00:43,021
Insights answers the same question properly.

7
00:00:43,081 --> 00:00:57,550
Where will it break first — every
node ranked by its binding constraint.

8
00:00:57,610 --> 00:01:15,658
Set the growth rate and the horizon,
and it tells you the month it saturates.

9
00:01:15,718 --> 00:01:24,666
Load is the twenty-four hour
profile, summed across every source.

10
00:01:24,726 --> 00:01:41,735
Daily total, peak per minute, and
which source contributes what.

11
00:01:41,795 --> 00:01:48,738
Compare puts two designs side by side
— cost, latency, storage, bottlenecks.

12
00:01:48,798 --> 00:02:05,556
On-prem against Azure, or
last week against this week.

13
00:02:05,616 --> 00:02:10,159
And Report is the version you send upstairs.

14
00:02:10,219 --> 00:02:29,788
Overview, diagram, nodes and costs, scaling
risk, resilience, data centre, multi-year TCO.

15
00:02:29,848 --> 00:02:33,790
None of that is a spreadsheet you maintain.

16
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:37,653
It is the design, answering.
